IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `twhs-dates`
IMPORT `twhs-rules`

-- Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) -- the questions of the WORKER.
--
-- SEAT: the worker (or prospective worker) who is hurt, put at risk, made to stop unsafe work, or treated badly for
-- raising safety, and who brings or presses a claim about it -- or, where a worker has died, the family member who asks
-- on the worker's behalf. "Am I a worker?", "Is my injury notifiable?", "May I stop this work?", "Can I take them to
-- court for sacking me over a safety complaint, and by when?", "What offence did they commit and what can they be
-- fined?", "Can I ask for a prosecution?", "Does the Act let me sue?". The business, the regulator, inspectors and the
-- courts get no seat: their judgments and findings are asked as facts.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, dates, numbers, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of twhs-rules.l4.
-- The time bars are DEONTIC rules run inside the questions that ask about a day or an hour; no question takes a DEONTIC
-- or an EVENT.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (c) State of Tasmania, CC BY
-- 4.0; not the authorised version. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas): the worker's questions`

§§ `Where I stand`

@export Am I a "worker" under the Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 7? You are if you carry out work in any capacity (employee, contractor, labour hire, outworker, apprentice, student on work experience, volunteer) for a business or undertaking, whether or not it is run for profit -- but not for a volunteer association that employs no one (s 5(7)). Police officers are workers, and so is an individual who runs the business and works in it.
GIVEN `do I carry out work for a business or undertaking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you carry out work in any capacity for a person or organisation conducting a business or undertaking (for profit or not), including as an employee, contractor, subcontractor, labour hire worker, outworker, apprentice, trainee, student gaining work experience or volunteer
      `is it a volunteer association with no employees` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation is a group of volunteers working together for community purposes in which no volunteer employs anyone to work for it
      `am I a police officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a police officer
      `do I run the business and work in it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an individual who conducts the business or undertaking yourself and carry out work in it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a worker` MEANS
    `s 7 -- I am a worker` `do I carry out work for a business or undertaking` `is it a volunteer association with no employees` `am I a police officer` `do I run the business and work in it`

@export Does the business owe me the primary duty of care to ensure my health and safety so far as is reasonably practicable (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 19)? It does to workers it engaged or caused to be engaged, and to workers whose work it influences or directs, while they are at work in the business; and it must ensure that other people's health and safety is not put at risk by its work. Whether it met the duty is a separate question for a court.
GIVEN `did the business engage me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business engaged you, or caused you to be engaged (for example through a labour hire firm or a contractor)
      `does the business direct my work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business influences or directs your activities in carrying out work
      `was I at work in the business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were at work in the business or undertaking at the time
      `could its work put me at risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether work carried out as part of the business could put your health and safety at risk (for a visitor, customer, member of the public or other person)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the business owe me the primary duty of care` MEANS
    `s 19 -- the business owes me the primary duty of care` `did the business engage me` `does the business direct my work` `was I at work in the business` `could its work put me at risk`

@export Must I follow this safety instruction (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 28(c))? While at work you must comply, so far as you are reasonably able, with any reasonable instruction the business gives to allow it to comply with the Act.
GIVEN `am I at work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are at work
      `did the business give it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the instruction was given by the business or undertaking you work for
      `is it reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the instruction is reasonable
      `is it to allow the business to comply with the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was given to allow the business to comply with the Work Health and Safety Act
      `am I reasonably able to comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are reasonably able to comply with it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I follow this instruction` MEANS
    `s 28(c) -- I must comply with the instruction` `am I at work` `did the business give it` `is it reasonable` `is it to allow the business to comply with the Act` `am I reasonably able to comply`

§§ `When I am hurt or put at risk: notifiable incidents`

@export Is my injury or illness a "serious injury or illness" under the Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 36? It is if it needs immediate treatment as a hospital in-patient, immediate treatment for an amputation, serious head or eye injury, serious burn, degloving or scalping, spinal injury, loss of a bodily function or serious lacerations, or medical treatment within 48 hours of exposure to a substance. Regulations (not encoded) may add or exclude kinds, so for other injuries this encoding cannot say.
GIVEN `what treatment did it need` IS A `Treatment required` @desc The treatment the injury or illness required. One of: "immediate treatment as an in-patient in a hospital", "immediate treatment for the amputation of part of the body", "immediate treatment for a serious head injury", "immediate treatment for a serious eye injury", "immediate treatment for a serious burn", "immediate treatment for degloving or scalping", "immediate treatment for a spinal injury", "immediate treatment for the loss of a bodily function", "immediate treatment for serious lacerations", "medical treatment within 48 hours of exposure to a substance", "other treatment, or none", "no injury or illness"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my injury a serious injury or illness` MEANS
    `s 36 -- it is a serious injury or illness` `what treatment did it need`

@export Was what happened a "dangerous incident" under the Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 37? It is an incident in relation to a workplace that exposed someone to a serious risk to health or safety from an immediate or imminent exposure to one of the listed events (an uncontrolled escape, spill or leak of a substance, an explosion or fire, escaping gas, steam or pressurised substance, electric shock, something falling from a height, collapse or failure of authorised plant, a structure or an excavation, an inrush in a tunnel or loss of its ventilation).
GIVEN `was it an incident in relation to a workplace` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident happened in relation to a workplace (any place where work is carried out for a business, including vehicles and vessels)
      `was someone exposed to a serious risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a worker or anyone else was exposed to a serious risk to their health or safety
      `was the exposure immediate or imminent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk came from an immediate or imminent exposure (not a long-term or gradual one)
      `what was the exposure to` IS AN `Exposure` @desc What the exposure was to. One of: "an uncontrolled escape, spillage or leakage of a substance", "an uncontrolled implosion, explosion or fire", "an uncontrolled escape of gas or steam", "an uncontrolled escape of a pressurised substance", "electric shock", "the fall or release from a height of any plant, substance or thing", "the collapse, overturning, failure or malfunction of, or damage to, plant that must be authorised", "the collapse or partial collapse of a structure", "the collapse or failure of an excavation or its shoring", "the inrush of water, mud or gas in workings, an underground excavation or tunnel", "the interruption of the main ventilation in an underground excavation or tunnel", "another kind of event", "no exposure"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was it a dangerous incident` MEANS
    `s 37 -- it is a dangerous incident` `was it an incident in relation to a workplace` `was someone exposed to a serious risk` `was the exposure immediate or imminent` `what was the exposure to`

@export Must the business notify the work health and safety regulator immediately of what happened to me (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) ss 35-38(1))? It must, after becoming aware of it, if a notifiable incident (a death, a serious injury or illness, or a dangerous incident) arose out of the conduct of its business; by telephone or in writing, by the fastest possible means.
GIVEN `did someone die` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person died in the incident
      `what treatment did the injury need` IS A `Treatment required` @desc The treatment the injury or illness required (see the serious injury question for the choices; "no injury or illness" if none)
      `was it in relation to a workplace` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident happened in relation to a workplace
      `was anyone exposed to a serious risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a worker or anyone else was exposed to a serious risk to their health or safety
      `was it immediate or imminent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that risk came from an immediate or imminent exposure
      `what was it an exposure to` IS AN `Exposure` @desc What the exposure was to (see the dangerous incident question for the choices; "no exposure" if none)
      `did it arise out of the business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident arose out of the conduct of the business or undertaking you ask about
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the business notify the regulator` MEANS
    `s 38(1) -- the business must notify the regulator` (`s 35 -- it is a notifiable incident` `did someone die` (`s 36 -- it is a serious injury or illness` `what treatment did the injury need`) (`s 37 -- it is a dangerous incident` `was it in relation to a workplace` `was anyone exposed to a serious risk` `was it immediate or imminent` `what was it an exposure to`)) `did it arise out of the business`

@export Was the written notice of my incident given in time (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 38(4)(b))? If the business notified by telephone and the regulator required a written notice, it had to be given within 48 hours of the requirement.
GIVEN `did the regulator require a written notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulator required the business to give a written notice of the incident after a telephone notice
      `has the written notice been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business has given the written notice
      `hours since the requirement` IS A NUMBER @desc Hours from the regulator's requirement to the written notice (or, if none has been given, to now)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was the written notice given in time` MEANS
    `s 38(4)(b) -- the written notice was given in time` `did the regulator require a written notice` `has the written notice been given` `hours since the requirement`

@export Until what day must the business keep its record of my notifiable incident (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 38(7))? For at least 5 years from the day the regulator was notified.
GIVEN `day the regulator was notified` IS A DATE @desc The day notice of the incident was given to the regulator
GIVETH A DATE
`Until when must the record of my incident be kept` MEANS
    `s 38(7) -- the last day the record must be kept` `day the regulator was notified`

@export Does the Act forbid disturbing the site of my incident for this action (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 39)? After a notifiable incident the site must, so far as is reasonably practicable, be left undisturbed until an inspector arrives or an earlier time an inspector directs -- but not so as to stop helping an injured person, removing a deceased person, making the site safe, a police investigation, or anything an inspector or the regulator permits.
GIVEN `was it a notifiable incident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident was a death, a serious injury or illness, or a dangerous incident (see those questions)
      `has an inspector arrived or the directed time come` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an inspector has arrived at the site, or the earlier time an inspector directed has come
      `what is the action for` IS A `Purpose of the action` @desc What the action is for. One of: "to assist an injured person", "to remove a deceased person", "to make the site safe or minimise the risk of a further notifiable incident", "associated with a police investigation", "permitted by an inspector or the regulator", "another purpose"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the Act forbid disturbing the incident site` MEANS
    `s 39 -- the site must not be disturbed by the action` (IF `was it a notifiable incident` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE) `has an inspector arrived or the directed time come` `what is the action for`

§§ `Unsafe work (only if you have stopped or been told to stop unsafe work)`

@export May I cease, or refuse to carry out, this work (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 84)? A worker may if they have a reasonable concern that carrying it out would expose them to a serious risk to their health or safety from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard.
GIVEN `am I a worker here` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker of the business (see the worker question)
      `is my concern reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a reasonable concern about carrying out the work
      `would it expose me to a serious risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether carrying out the work would expose you to a serious risk to your health or safety
      `is the hazard immediate or imminent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk comes from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I cease or refuse this work` MEANS
    `s 84 -- I may cease or refuse the work` `am I a worker here` `is my concern reasonable` `would it expose me to a serious risk` `is the hazard immediate or imminent`

@export Could the health and safety representative validly direct me to cease work (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 85)? The representative of your work group may, if they have the required training and a reasonable concern of a serious risk from an immediate or imminent hazard, after consulting the business and trying to resolve the issue -- or without that, if the risk is so serious and immediate that consulting first is not reasonable.
GIVEN `am I in their work group` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are in the work group the health and safety representative represents
      `did they have a reasonable concern` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative had a reasonable concern that the work would expose you to a serious risk to your health or safety from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard
      `are they trained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative has completed the prescribed initial training (now or for another work group) or equivalent training under a corresponding WHS law
      `did they consult and try to resolve it first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative consulted the business and tried to resolve the matter under the issue resolution procedure, and it was not resolved
      `was it too urgent to consult` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk was so serious and immediate or imminent that it was not reasonable to consult before giving the direction
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Could the representative direct me to cease work` MEANS
    `s 85 -- the representative could direct me to cease work` `am I in their work group` `did they have a reasonable concern` `are they trained` `did they consult and try to resolve it first` `was it too urgent to consult`

@export After I ceased unsafe work, must I tell the business that I have done so (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 86)? Yes, as soon as practicable, if you stopped of your own accord under s 84; not if you stopped on a health and safety representative's direction. Either way you must remain available for suitable alternative work.
GIVEN `how did I stop` IS A `How I ceased work` @desc How you stopped work. One of: "I ceased or refused the work myself (s 84)", "on a direction of a health and safety representative (s 85)"
      `am I a worker there` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker of the business
      `was my concern reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you stopped yourself: whether you had a reasonable concern of a serious risk from an immediate or imminent hazard
      `was the risk serious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you stopped yourself: whether carrying out the work would have exposed you to a serious risk to your health or safety
      `was the hazard immediate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you stopped yourself: whether the risk came from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard
      `am I in the representative's group` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If directed: whether you are in the representative's work group
      `did the representative have a reasonable concern` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If directed: whether the representative had a reasonable concern of a serious risk from an immediate or imminent hazard
      `is the representative trained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If directed: whether the representative has the required training
      `did the representative consult first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If directed: whether the representative consulted the business and tried issue resolution without success
      `was it too urgent for consultation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If directed: whether the risk was too serious and immediate to consult first
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I tell the business I ceased work` MEANS
    `s 86(a) -- I must notify the business that I ceased work` `how did I stop` (`s 83 -- I ceased work under the Division` `how did I stop` (`s 84 -- I may cease or refuse the work` `am I a worker there` `was my concern reasonable` `was the risk serious` `was the hazard immediate`) (`s 85 -- the representative could direct me to cease work` `am I in the representative's group` `did the representative have a reasonable concern` `is the representative trained` `did the representative consult first` `was it too urgent for consultation`))

@export Does ceasing unsafe work leave the continuity of my engagement unaffected (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 88)? It does, for the purposes the regulations prescribe, if you ceased work lawfully under s 84 or s 85 and did not unreasonably fail to follow a direction to do suitable alternative work that was safe and appropriate for you.
GIVEN `how I stopped` IS A `How I ceased work` @desc How you stopped work. One of: "I ceased or refused the work myself (s 84)", "on a direction of a health and safety representative (s 85)"
      `am I a worker of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker of the business
      `was my concern a reasonable one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you stopped yourself: whether you had a reasonable concern of a serious risk from an immediate or imminent hazard
      `was the risk a serious one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you stopped yourself: whether carrying out the work would have exposed you to a serious risk to your health or safety
      `was the hazard immediate or imminent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you stopped yourself: whether the risk came from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard
      `am I in that representative's group` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If directed: whether you are in the representative's work group
      `had the representative a reasonable concern` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If directed: whether the representative had a reasonable concern of a serious risk from an immediate or imminent hazard
      `has the representative the training` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If directed: whether the representative has the required training
      `did the representative consult and try to resolve it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If directed: whether the representative consulted the business and tried issue resolution without success
      `was there no time to consult` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If directed: whether the risk was too serious and immediate to consult first
      `was I directed to safe alternative work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business directed you to carry out suitable alternative work, at the same or another workplace, that was safe and appropriate for you
      `did I unreasonably fail to do it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you unreasonably failed to comply with that direction
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the continuity of my engagement unaffected` MEANS
    `s 88 -- my continuity of engagement is not affected` (`s 83 -- I ceased work under the Division` `how I stopped` (`s 84 -- I may cease or refuse the work` `am I a worker of it` `was my concern a reasonable one` `was the risk a serious one` `was the hazard immediate or imminent`) (`s 85 -- the representative could direct me to cease work` `am I in that representative's group` `had the representative a reasonable concern` `has the representative the training` `did the representative consult and try to resolve it` `was there no time to consult`)) `was I directed to safe alternative work` `did I unreasonably fail to do it`

§§ `Treated badly for raising safety (only if something was done to you, or threatened, over a safety matter)`

@export Was what was done to me "discriminatory conduct" for a "prohibited reason" (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) ss 105, 106)? Discriminatory conduct is dismissing you, ending your contract, putting you to detriment or altering your position to your detriment, refusing to engage you or offering you worse terms, or ending or refusing a commercial arrangement with you -- or organising or threatening any of these. It is for a prohibited reason if done because of your safety role, your use of powers or functions under the Act, your help to an inspector, your raising a safety concern with a listed person, your part in resolving a safety issue, or your seeking compliance with the Act.
GIVEN `what did they do` IS A `What was done` @desc What was done to you, or organised or threatened. One of: "I was dismissed", "my contract for services was terminated", "I was put to my detriment in my engagement", "my position was altered to my detriment", "I was refused or not offered engagement as a prospective worker", "I was offered less favourable terms than another prospective worker", "a commercial arrangement with me was terminated", "a commercial arrangement with me was refused or not entered into", "something else"
      `why did they do it` IS A `Why it was done` @desc Why it was done. One of: "my being a health and safety representative or committee member", "my undertaking another role under the Act", "my exercising powers or functions as a representative or committee member", "my exercising, or not exercising, a power under the Act", "my performing, or not performing, a function under the Act", "my assisting or giving information to a person exercising a power or function under the Act", "my raising a health and safety issue or concern with a person s 106(h) lists" (the business, an inspector, a WHS entry permit holder, a health and safety representative or committee member, another worker, another duty holder, or anyone exercising powers under the Act), "my raising a health and safety issue or concern with someone else", "my involvement in resolving a health and safety issue under the Act", "my taking action to seek compliance with a duty or obligation under the Act", "another reason"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was it discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason` MEANS
    `ss 105, 106 -- it is discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason` `what did they do` `why did they do it`

@export Can I apply to the Magistrates Court (Civil Division) for orders about discriminatory or coercive conduct against me (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) ss 112, 113(1), 115)? A person affected (or their authorised representative) may, within 1 year after the day they knew or ought to have known the cause of action accrued -- but not while another proceeding or complaint about the same matter under Commonwealth or State law is on foot, or after failing in one (other than a workers compensation one).
GIVEN `am I affected or authorised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a person affected by the conduct, or authorised as a representative by a person affected
      `day I knew or ought to have known` IS A DATE @desc The day you knew, or ought to have known, that the cause of action accrued (usually, that the conduct happened)
      `day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you start, or would start, the proceeding
      `is another proceeding on the same matter on foot` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have started a proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under another Commonwealth or State law (for example unfair dismissal, general protections or anti-discrimination) that has not been withdrawn
      `did I fail in another proceeding on the same matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed in a proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under another Commonwealth or State law, other than one about workers compensation
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to the Magistrates Court` MEANS
    `s 112 -- I may apply to the Magistrates Court` `am I affected or authorised` `day I knew or ought to have known` `day I apply` `is another proceeding on the same matter on foot` `did I fail in another proceeding on the same matter`

@export By what day must I start my civil proceeding about discriminatory or coercive conduct (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 113(1))? Not more than 1 year after the day you knew or ought to have known that the cause of action accrued.
GIVEN `the day I knew or should have known` IS A DATE @desc The day you knew, or ought to have known, that the cause of action accrued
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I start my civil proceeding` MEANS
    `s 113(1) -- the last day to commence` `the day I knew or should have known`

@export Can the Magistrates Court make this order for me about discriminatory or coercive conduct (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) ss 112-115)? On a proceeding started in time, the court may grant an injunction or any other appropriate order; compensation for discriminatory conduct (or for inducing it); and reinstatement, re-employment or employment for an employee or prospective employee who suffered it. A prohibited reason is presumed to be a substantial reason unless the other side proves otherwise; it is a defence that the conduct was reasonable and mainly to comply with work health and safety law; and there is no order if a criminal court already made a compensation or reinstatement order for the same conduct, and no compensation if you were compensated under another law.
GIVEN `the order` IS AN `Order` @desc The order you want. One of: "an injunction", "compensation", "reinstatement, re-employment or employment", "any other order the court considers appropriate"
      `my complaint` IS A `Kind of complaint` @desc What you complain of. One of: "discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason", "asking, instructing, inducing, encouraging, authorising or assisting another to engage in it", "coercion or inducement contrary to s 108"
      `am I affected by it or authorised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a person affected by the conduct, or authorised as a representative by a person affected
      `day I knew or ought to have known of it` IS A DATE @desc The day you knew, or ought to have known, that the cause of action accrued
      `day I start` IS A DATE @desc The day you start, or started, the proceeding
      `is another proceeding on foot` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under another Commonwealth or State law has been started and not withdrawn
      `did I fail elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed in a proceeding, application or complaint about the same matter under another law, other than workers compensation
      `what was done to me` IS A `What was done` @desc For discriminatory conduct: what was done, organised or threatened (see the discriminatory conduct question for the choices)
      `why it was done to me` IS A `Why it was done` @desc For discriminatory conduct: why it was done (see the discriminatory conduct question for the choices)
      `did they prove it was not a substantial reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other side proved, on the balance of probabilities, that the prohibited reason was not a substantial reason for the conduct
      `did they prove the defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other side proved that the conduct was reasonable in the circumstances and that a substantial reason for it was to comply with the Act or a corresponding WHS law
      `was action taken or threatened against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For coercion: whether action was taken or threatened against you (including not taking an action)
      `was it meant to coerce me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For coercion: whether it was meant to coerce or induce you (or someone else) to use or not use a power, perform or not perform a function, or give up a role, under the Act
      `was it an emergency direction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For coercion: whether it was a reasonable direction given by an emergency services worker in an emergency
      `did a criminal court already order compensation or reinstatement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a criminal court has already made a compensation or reinstatement order under s 111 for the same conduct
      `was I compensated under another law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received compensation for the matter under another Commonwealth or State law
      `am I an employee or prospective employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were or are an employee, or a prospective employee, of the person (not, for example, an independent contractor)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the court make this order for me` MEANS
    `s 112(3) -- the court may make the order for me` (`s 112 -- I may apply to the Magistrates Court` `am I affected by it or authorised` `day I knew or ought to have known of it` `day I start` `is another proceeding on foot` `did I fail elsewhere`) (`Civil case` WITH
            `the complaint` IS `my complaint`
            `what was done` IS `what was done to me`
            `why it was done` IS `why it was done to me`
            `the defendant proved the reason was not a substantial reason` IS `did they prove it was not a substantial reason`
            `the defendant proved the conduct was reasonable and mainly to comply with WHS law` IS `did they prove the defence`
            `coercion contravention` IS `s 108 -- the person contravened the coercion prohibition` `was action taken or threatened against me` `was it meant to coerce me` `was it an emergency direction`
            `a s 111 order was made for the same conduct` IS `did a criminal court already order compensation or reinstatement`
            `I was compensated for the matter under another law` IS `was I compensated under another law`
            `I was or am an employee or prospective employee` IS `am I an employee or prospective employee`) `the order`

@export If the person who discriminated against me is convicted, can the criminal court also order compensation or my reinstatement (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) ss 111, 114(2))? On a conviction under s 104 or 107 the court may order compensation, and for an employee or prospective employee reinstatement, re-employment or employment -- unless the Magistrates Court already made an order under s 112 for the same conduct.
GIVEN `the order I want` IS AN `Order` @desc The order you want. One of: "compensation", "reinstatement, re-employment or employment" (an injunction or other order is not given by s 111)
      `were they convicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was convicted or found guilty of an offence under s 104 or s 107 for the conduct
      `was a s 112 order made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Magistrates Court has already made an order under s 112 for the same conduct
      `am I an employee or applicant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were or are an employee or prospective employee of the person
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the criminal court order compensation or reinstatement for me` MEANS
    `s 111 -- the criminal court may make the order for me` `were they convicted` `was a s 112 order made` `am I an employee or applicant` `the order I want`

@export On these facts, is the offence of discriminatory conduct against me made out (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) ss 104, 110)? It is an offence to engage in discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason that was the dominant reason; once the conduct and the circumstance are proved and there is evidence of the reason, it is presumed to be the dominant reason unless the accused proves it was not.
GIVEN `what they did` IS A `What was done` @desc What was done, organised or threatened (see the discriminatory conduct question for the choices)
      `why they did it` IS A `Why it was done` @desc Why it was done (see the discriminatory conduct question for the choices)
      `is there evidence of that reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is evidence that the conduct was engaged in for that reason
      `did the accused prove it was not the dominant reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the accused proved, on the balance of probabilities, that the reason was not the dominant reason
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the discrimination offence made out` MEANS
    `s 104 -- an offence of discriminatory conduct is made out` `what they did` `why they did it` `is there evidence of that reason` `did the accused prove it was not the dominant reason`

@export Was the false or misleading thing I was told about my work health and safety rights prohibited (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 109)? No one may knowingly or recklessly make a false or misleading representation to you about your rights or obligations under the Act, your ability to start or take part in a process or proceeding under it, or your ability to complain to the regulator -- unless you would not be expected to rely on it.
GIVEN `was it false or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what you were told was false or misleading
      `was it knowing or reckless` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person made it knowingly or recklessly
      `was it about my WHS rights, processes or complaints` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was about your rights or obligations under the Act, your ability to start or take part in a process or proceeding under it, or your ability to complain or inquire to someone who enforces it
      `would I not be expected to rely on it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would not be expected to rely on it (for example, an obvious joke)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was the representation to me prohibited` MEANS
    `s 109 -- the representation to me was prohibited` `was it false or misleading` `was it knowing or reckless` `was it about my WHS rights, processes or complaints` `would I not be expected to rely on it`

@export What is the most the person can be fined, in dollars, for this contravention that affects me (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) ss 38, 39, 104, 107, 108, 109, 273)?
GIVEN `the contravention` IS A `Contravention` @desc The contravention. One of: "not notifying the regulator of a notifiable incident (s 38(1))", "not keeping the record of a notifiable incident (s 38(7))", "disturbing an incident site (s 39(1))", "discriminatory conduct (s 104)", "requesting, inducing or assisting discriminatory conduct (s 107)", "coercion or inducement (s 108)", "misrepresentation about my rights (s 109)", "levying or charging me for work health and safety (s 273)"
      `who did it` IS AN `Offender` @desc Who committed it. One of: "an individual", "a body corporate"
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most they can be fined for this contravention` MEANS
    `the maximum fine for the contravention` `the contravention` `by` `who did it`

§§ `Offences against the duty owed to me (only if a duty holder's failure exposed you to risk, hurt you, or caused a death)`

@export What is the most serious offence under Part 2 of the Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) that these facts make out against the person who owed me (or my family member who died) a health and safety duty: industrial manslaughter (s 29C), a Category 1 (s 31), Category 2 (s 32) or Category 3 (s 33) offence, or none? Volunteers are liable only for the worker's and other person's duties, and unincorporated associations not at all (s 34). Whether each fact would be proved is for a court.
GIVEN `who owed the duty` IS A `Duty holder` @desc Who owed the duty. One of: "an individual conducting the business or undertaking, or an officer of it", "a body corporate conducting the business or undertaking", "another individual, such as a worker or other person at the workplace", "an unincorporated association"
      `were they a volunteer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the duty holder was a volunteer
      `was it a worker's or other person's duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the duty was the duty of a worker (s 28) or of another person at the workplace (s 29)
      `did they owe a duty to the individual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they owed a health and safety duty under Part 2 to the individual (you, or the family member who died)
      `did they fail to comply with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they failed to comply with that duty
      `was the individual exposed to a risk of death or serious harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their conduct or failure exposed the individual to a risk of death or serious injury or illness
      `had they no reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they engaged in that conduct without reasonable excuse
      `were they reckless as to that risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they were reckless as to the risk to the individual of death or serious injury or illness
      `did the individual die` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the individual died, or was injured and later died
      `did their conduct substantially contribute to the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their conduct substantially contributed to the death
      `did they fall greatly short of reasonable care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their conduct fell greatly short of the standard of care a reasonable person (or reasonable body corporate) would have taken in the circumstances
      `was there a high risk of death or serious harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their conduct involved a high risk of death, serious injury or serious illness
GIVETH AN `Offence made out`
`Which offence do these facts make out` MEANS
    `the most serious offence made out` (`Duty case` WITH
        `the holder` IS `who owed the duty`
        `the holder is a volunteer` IS `were they a volunteer`
        `the duty is a duty under s 28 or 29` IS `was it a worker's or other person's duty`
        `a duty was owed` IS `did they owe a duty to the individual`
        `the duty was not complied with` IS `did they fail to comply with it`
        `it exposed the individual to a risk of death or serious injury or illness` IS `was the individual exposed to a risk of death or serious harm`
        `the holder had no reasonable excuse` IS `had they no reasonable excuse`
        `the holder was reckless as to that risk` IS `were they reckless as to that risk`
        `the individual died` IS `did the individual die`
        `the conduct substantially contributed to the death` IS `did their conduct substantially contribute to the death`
        `the conduct fell greatly short of the standard of care of a reasonable person` IS `did they fall greatly short of reasonable care`
        `the conduct involved a high risk of death or serious injury or illness` IS `was there a high risk of death or serious harm`)

@export What is the maximum fine, in dollars, for this Part 2 offence against the duty owed to me (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) ss 29C, 31, 32, 33)? None is answered where the Act sets no fine (industrial manslaughter by an individual is punished by imprisonment) or the person cannot commit the offence.
GIVEN `which offence` IS AN `Offence made out` @desc The offence. One of: "industrial manslaughter (s 29C)", "a Category 1 offence (s 31)", "a Category 2 offence (s 32)", "a Category 3 offence (s 33)", "no offence under Part 2 on these facts"
      `who is the offender` IS A `Duty holder` @desc Who committed it. One of: "an individual conducting the business or undertaking, or an officer of it", "a body corporate conducting the business or undertaking", "another individual, such as a worker or other person at the workplace", "an unincorporated association"
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the maximum fine for the offence` MEANS
    `the maximum fine for` `which offence` `by` `who is the offender`

@export What is the longest prison term, in years, for this Part 2 offence against the duty owed to me (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) ss 29C, 31)? 21 years for industrial manslaughter by an individual running the business or an officer of it; 5 years for a Category 1 offence by an individual; none otherwise.
GIVEN `what offence` IS AN `Offence made out` @desc The offence (see the maximum fine question for the choices)
      `who offended` IS A `Duty holder` @desc Who committed it (see the maximum fine question for the choices)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the longest prison term for the offence` MEANS
    `the maximum years of imprisonment for` `what offence` `by` `who offended`

§§ `Getting a prosecution brought (only if no prosecution has been brought)`

@export Can I ask the regulator in writing to bring a prosecution (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 231(1))? You may if you reasonably consider that what happened was industrial manslaughter or a Category 1 or Category 2 offence, and no prosecution has been brought, from after 6 months until 12 months after it happened.
GIVEN `what offence do I consider it was` IS AN `Offence I consider was committed` @desc The offence you consider was committed. One of: "industrial manslaughter", "a Category 1 offence", "a Category 2 offence", "a Category 3 offence or another offence against the Act"
      `do I reasonably consider so` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you reasonably consider that what happened constitutes that offence (you may take a coroner's report or inquest into account)
      `has a prosecution been brought` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a prosecution has been brought for it
      `day it happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the act, matter, thing or failure occurred
      `day of my request` IS A DATE @desc The day you make, or would make, the written request
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I ask for a prosecution` MEANS
    `s 231(1) -- I may request a prosecution` `what offence do I consider it was` `do I reasonably consider so` `has a prosecution been brought` `day it happened` `day of my request`

@export By what day must the regulator give me its written advice on my request for a prosecution (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 231(2))? Within 3 months after it receives the request: whether the investigation is complete and, if so, whether a prosecution has been or will be brought or why not.
GIVEN `day the regulator received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the regulator received your written request
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the regulator advise me` MEANS
    `s 231(2) -- the last day for the regulator's advice` `day the regulator received my request`

@export Did the regulator give me its written advice on my request for a prosecution in time (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 231(2))?
GIVEN `day it received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the regulator received your written request
      `has it advised me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulator has given you its written advice
      `day of its advice or today` IS A DATE @desc The day of the advice, or today if none has been given
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did the regulator advise me in time` MEANS
    `s 231(2) -- the regulator advised me in time` `day it received my request` `has it advised me` `day of its advice or today`

@export By what day must the regulator refer my matter to the Director of Public Prosecutions (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 231(3))? If it advised that a prosecution for a Category 1 or Category 2 offence will not be brought and you asked in writing for a referral, within 1 month of your request. The section does not mention industrial manslaughter.
GIVEN `which offence was it` IS AN `Offence I consider was committed` @desc The offence your request was about (see the prosecution request question for the choices)
      `did the regulator say no prosecution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulator advised you that a prosecution will not be brought
      `day I asked for a referral` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked the regulator in writing to refer the matter to the Director of Public Prosecutions
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must my matter be referred to the DPP` MEANS
    `s 231(3)(b) -- the last day to refer my matter to the DPP` `which offence was it` `did the regulator say no prosecution` `day I asked for a referral`

@export By when can the person who harmed me or put me at risk be prosecuted (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 232)? Within the latest of: 2 years after the offence first came to the regulator's notice; 1 year after a coroner's report or inquest that showed an offence; 6 months after a WHS undertaking about it was breached (or the breach came to the regulator's notice) or withdrawn. A Category 1 offence may be prosecuted later on fresh evidence; industrial manslaughter has no time limit, but cannot be prosecuted while Criminal Code charges for the same conduct stand.
GIVEN `which offence is it` IS AN `Offence I consider was committed` @desc The offence (see the prosecution request question for the choices)
      `day the regulator learned of it` IS A DATE @desc The day the offence first came to the regulator's notice
      `did a coroner find an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appeared from a coroner's report, or an inquiry or inquest, that an offence against the Act had been committed
      `day of the coroner's report or end of inquest` IS A DATE @desc The day the coroner's report was made or the inquiry or inquest ended (any date if none)
      `was an undertaking breached or withdrawn` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a WHS undertaking was given about the offence and was then breached, or withdrawn with the regulator's agreement
      `latest day of breach, its notice or withdrawal` IS A DATE @desc The latest of: the day it was breached, the day the breach came to the regulator's notice, and the day the regulator agreed to its withdrawal (any date if none)
      `is there fresh evidence for a Category 1 offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a Category 1 offence: whether fresh evidence has been discovered that the court is satisfied could not reasonably have been discovered in time
      `do Criminal Code charges stand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For industrial manslaughter: whether charges under the Criminal Code have been laid for the same conduct and not withdrawn or dismissed
GIVETH A `Prosecution time`
`By when can they be prosecuted` MEANS
    `s 232 -- the time for prosecution` (`Prosecution facts` WITH
        `the offence charged` IS `which offence is it`
        `the day it came to the regulator's notice` IS `day the regulator learned of it`
        `a coronial report or inquest showed an offence` IS `did a coroner find an offence`
        `the day the report was made or the inquest ended` IS `day of the coroner's report or end of inquest`
        `a WHS undertaking was given and later breached or withdrawn` IS `was an undertaking breached or withdrawn`
        `the latest day of breach, its notice or withdrawal` IS `latest day of breach, its notice or withdrawal`
        `fresh evidence the court accepts for a Category 1 offence` IS `is there fresh evidence for a Category 1 offence`
        `Criminal Code charges for the same conduct stand` IS `do Criminal Code charges stand`)

@export Can the person who harmed me or put me at risk still be prosecuted on this day (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 232)?
GIVEN `the offence it is` IS AN `Offence I consider was committed` @desc The offence (see the prosecution request question for the choices)
      `day it came to the regulator's notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the offence first came to the regulator's notice
      `did a coroner's report show an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appeared from a coroner's report, inquiry or inquest that an offence had been committed
      `day of the report or inquest end` IS A DATE @desc The day the report was made or the inquiry or inquest ended (any date if none)
      `was a WHS undertaking breached or withdrawn` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a WHS undertaking about the offence was breached or withdrawn
      `latest undertaking day` IS A DATE @desc The latest of the day of breach, the day the breach came to the regulator's notice and the day of withdrawal (any date if none)
      `is there accepted fresh evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a Category 1 offence: whether fresh evidence the court accepts could not reasonably have been discovered in time
      `are Criminal Code charges standing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For industrial manslaughter: whether Criminal Code charges for the same conduct have been laid and not withdrawn or dismissed
      `the day of prosecution` IS A DATE @desc The day the prosecution would be brought
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can they still be prosecuted on this day` MEANS
    `s 232 -- a prosecution on the day is in time` (`s 232 -- the time for prosecution` (`Prosecution facts` WITH
        `the offence charged` IS `the offence it is`
        `the day it came to the regulator's notice` IS `day it came to the regulator's notice`
        `a coronial report or inquest showed an offence` IS `did a coroner's report show an offence`
        `the day the report was made or the inquest ended` IS `day of the report or inquest end`
        `a WHS undertaking was given and later breached or withdrawn` IS `was a WHS undertaking breached or withdrawn`
        `the latest day of breach, its notice or withdrawal` IS `latest undertaking day`
        `fresh evidence the court accepts for a Category 1 offence` IS `is there accepted fresh evidence`
        `Criminal Code charges for the same conduct stand` IS `are Criminal Code charges standing`)) `the day of prosecution`

§§ `My rights against the business and the State`

@export Does the Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) itself give me a right to sue over this contravention (s 267)? Only for discriminatory or coercive conduct (Part 6, s 112). It gives no civil right of action for a breach of a health and safety duty or other provision (civil penalty proceedings are for the regulator or an inspector, s 260) -- but it also takes nothing away from your other civil rights, such as a negligence claim or workers compensation.
GIVEN `what was contravened` IS A `Provision contravened` @desc The provision contravened. One of: "discriminatory or coercive conduct (ss 104, 107, 108)", "misrepresentation (s 109)", "a WHS civil penalty provision (Part 7 or the regulations)", "any other provision, such as a health and safety duty in Part 2"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the Act give me a right to sue` MEANS
    `s 267 -- the Act gives me a civil right of action` `what was contravened`

@export Is this term of my contract or agreement void because it contracts out of the Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) (s 272)? A term that purports to exclude, limit or modify the Act or a duty under it, or to transfer a duty under it to someone else, is void.
GIVEN `does it exclude, limit or modify the Act or a duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the term purports to exclude, limit or modify the operation of the Act or any duty owed under it (for example, "the worker accepts all safety risks")
      `does it transfer a duty to someone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the term purports to transfer a duty owed under the Act to another person (for example, to you)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is this term of my contract void` MEANS
    `s 272 -- the term is void` `does it exclude, limit or modify the Act or a duty` `does it transfer a duty to someone else`

@export Can the business charge me for this (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 273)? A business must not impose, or permit, a levy or charge on a worker for anything done or provided in relation to work health and safety (such as protective equipment, training or a safety check).
GIVEN `am I its worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker of the business
      `is it for something done or provided for health and safety` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the levy or charge is for something done or provided in relation to work health and safety
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the business charge me for this` MEANS
    `s 273 -- the business may charge me` `am I its worker` `is it for something done or provided for health and safety`

@export Can a court order the State to compensate me for loss or expense caused by an inspector's entry, search, seizure or document powers (Work Health and Safety Act 2012 (Tas) s 184)? You may claim if the loss or expense came from the exercise (or purported exercise) of a power under Part 9 Division 3, in a court or in a prosecution against you; the court may order it only if satisfied it is just.
GIVEN `did an inspector's power cause my loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you incurred loss or expense because an inspector exercised, or purported to exercise, a power of entry, search, seizure, or to require documents or answers (Part 9 Division 3)
      `is the court satisfied it is just` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that it is just to order compensation in the circumstances of your case
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the court order the State to compensate me` MEANS
    `s 184 -- the court may order the State to compensate me` `did an inspector's power cause my loss` `is the court satisfied it is just`
